            <ArticleTitle>[Recurrent benign multicystic peritoneal mesothelioma: Approach to this rare condition].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>499-502</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0009-7411(15)00270-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.circir.2015.08.013</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Benign multicystic mesothelioma is a rare benign tumour derived from the peritoneal mesothelium. The aim of this paper is to present a case of this rare tumour and review the clinical features, diagnosis and treatment of this disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">The case is presented of a 22-year-old female diagnosed with multicystic mesothelioma after an urgent resection of intra-abdominal tumour in the context of acute abdominal pain. In the subsequent follow-up, the patient had a recurrence of the lesion, and at 2 years was treated by further resection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Benign multicystic mesothelioma is a benign tumour of unknown origin, and with a non-specific clinical manifestation. The most effective treatment is surgical, although there is a high tendency to local recurrence.</AbstractText>
